# Each phrase credits a claim to a source the sentence never names, so
# the fix names the source or drops the claim.
#
# `research shows` and `studies have shown` pass when a citation in
# parens or brackets follows (`Research shows (Smith 2021) that`). A
# bracket carries no part-of-speech tag, so the guard `!( !@tagged)`
# rejects a following word with any tag and keeps the bracket; a
# `PUNCT` or `SYM` guard never matches it. A citation at the end of the
# sentence still fires, since the guard reads only the next token.
#
# `industry reports` needs a finding verb after it, because the bare
# noun phrase names real filings (`industry reports are filed
# monthly`). `many believe` also fires on a question (`how many
# believe it?`), which technical prose rarely asks.
expr tagged [ADJ, ADP, ADV, AUX, CCONJ, DET, INTJ, NOUN, NUM, PART, PRON, PROPN, SCONJ, SYM, VERB, X]
expr claim [believe, say, agree, suggest, warn, recommend, argue, claim]
expr finding [show, shows, showed, suggest, suggests, indicate, indicates]
expr main [(experts @claim), (according to experts), (industry reports @finding), (some critics), (critics @claim), ([studies, research] @finding!( !@tagged)), ([studies, research] [has, have] shown!( !@tagged)), (many believe), (it is [widely, generally, commonly, often] [believed, known, said, accepted]), (is widely [regarded, considered, seen])]
let message "Vague attribution. Name the source or delete the claim."
let description "Flags a claim credited to unnamed experts, studies, or critics."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Research shows (Smith 2021) that review catches bugs."
allows "Industry reports are filed monthly to the regulator."
allows "The 2021 DORA report found that review catches bugs."
